<p>
  When you are dealing with a stubborn tendon issue, it is natural to look for anything that might speed up the recovery process. You might have come across silica — also called silicon — being mentioned in fitness or wellness circles as a secret weapon for connective tissue health.
</p>

<p>
  But does the science back up the hype? And should you add it to your recovery routine? Here is what I think is worth knowing.
</p>

<h2>The Role of Collagen in Tendon Healing</h2>

<p>
  Tendons are primarily made of Type I collagen — dense, organized proteins built to handle high mechanical loads. For a tendon to heal and remodel, your body needs the right chemical building blocks to synthesize and cross-link this collagen effectively.
</p>

<p>
  That is where certain forms of silica come into the picture. Research suggests that biologically active silicon helps activate the enzymes involved in collagen assembly, acting as a structural co-factor to support tissue resilience from the inside out.
</p>

<p>
  The key word there is <em>biologically active</em>.
</p>

<h2>Not All Silica Is Created Equal</h2>

<p>
  If you just grab a standard silica supplement off a health food store shelf, your body will absorb very little of it. To have any meaningful effect on your tendons, you need a highly bioavailable form. Two specific types have shown promise in clinical and preclinical research:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Choline-Stabilized Orthosilicic Acid (ch-OSA)</strong> — This formulation is stabilized so your digestive tract can actually absorb it. It has been studied for its ability to actively stimulate collagen synthesis pathways.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Monomethylsilanetriol (Organic Silicon)</strong> — Used in early-stage tissue models, this form has been studied for its ability to support fibroblast proliferation — the cells responsible for producing new tendon tissue.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  Both are a significant step up from the generic silica you find in most multi-mineral products.
</p>

<h2>Options Available in Canada</h2>

<p>
  If you are in Alberta and want to explore this, there are two reputable brands worth knowing about:
</p>

