</p>

<h2>The Part That Actually Builds the Tendon</h2>

<p>
  Here is what I want to be clear about: supplements can support the environment for healing, but they cannot do the healing on their own.
</p>

<blockquote>
  Think of silica as the raw materials delivered to a construction site. Structured, progressive loading is the blueprint that tells your body what to build — and where.
</blockquote>

<p>
  Tendons only remodel and grow stronger when they are subjected to the right kind of physical force. <a href="/blog/heavy-slow-resistance-training-achilles">Progressive mechanical loading</a> — like heavy slow resistance training — is what signals those new collagen building blocks to align properly and integrate into functional tissue.
</p>

<p>
  A supplement without a loading program is like stacking lumber in a field and hoping a house appears.
</p>

<h2>Is It Worth Adding?</h2>

<p>
  For most people, the evidence supporting bioavailable silica as a connective tissue supplement is promising but not definitive. It is low-risk, reasonably affordable, and may give your body a marginal advantage during active rehabilitation.
</p>

<p>
  That said, I would not prioritize it over sleep, adequate protein intake, or — most importantly — consistent loading work.
</p>

<p>
  If you are already doing the fundamentals well and want to add a nutritional edge, bioavailable silica is one of the more rational options out there. If you want to talk through whether it makes sense for your specific situation, <a href="/book">book a session</a> and we can build it into your plan.
</p>

